  "live_records": {
    "q-5435": {
      "dom_id": "step-q5435",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the $31$st term of an arithmetic sequence given that the $22$nd term is $76$ and the $6$th term is $-4.$",
      "worked_solution": "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{22}=76$ and ${a}_{6}=-4$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{22}-{a}_{6}}{22-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{76-(-4)}{22-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{80}{16} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{6}=-4,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{6}$ is $5$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}+5d \\\\ -4 & ={a}_{1}+5\\cdot 5 \\\\ -4 & ={a}_{1}+25 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-29+(n-1)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-34+5n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find the $31$st term by substituting $n=31$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31} & =-34+5(31) \\\\  & =-34+155 \\\\  & =121\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
